---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import release from '../../data/release-manifest.json';

const releaseLabel = release.hasRelease ? `v${release.version}` : 'the next release';
---

<Layout
  title="Varve on Chromebook — Varve Docs"
  description="How to run Varve on a Chromebook: the browser route, the installed web app, and the optional ARM64 Linux environment — with tested status, requirements, and limitations."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Varve on Chromebook
      </nav>

      <h1>Varve on Chromebook</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        ChromeOS is not one platform: Varve can run in Chrome, as an installed web app,
        or as a Linux application inside ChromeOS's Linux development environment. This
        page chooses between them honestly, including what is not verified yet.
      </p>

      <aside class="docs-note">
        <strong>Status:</strong> both Chromebook routes are <strong>experimental</strong>.
        The browser build's install, offline, and export behavior was tested against the
        production artifact on 2026-09-12; the Linux packages were verified byte-for-byte
        for {releaseLabel} on 2026-09-12. No physical Chromebook run has been recorded
        yet, so touch, pen, rendering, and suspend behavior on a device remain
        unverified. Nothing here is a supported-device claim.
      </aside>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Choose a route</h2>
        <div class="route-grid">
          <div class="route-card">
            <h3>1. Browser tab <span class="route-badge">no install</span></h3>
            <p>
              Open <a href={sitePath('/try/')}>varve.studio/try/</a> in Chrome. The editor
              runs on the WASM engine with browser storage. This is the lowest-setup route
              and works on any current Chromebook, including managed devices where Linux
              is unavailable.
            </p>
            <p class="route-meta">Best for: trying Varve, small documents, Chromebooks with Linux disabled.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="route-card">
            <h3>2. Installed web app <span class="route-badge">same build</span></h3>
            <p>
              From the same page, use Chrome's <strong>Install page as app</strong> action
              to get a standalone window and launcher icon. It shares storage with the
              tab, works offline after one online visit, and asks before applying an
              update. Installation can be blocked by an administrator on managed devices.
            </p>
            <p class="route-meta">Best for: everyday browser use without a visible tab bar.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="route-card">
            <h3>3. Linux application <span class="route-badge">separate route</span></h3>
            <p>
              If the Linux development environment is enabled, install the ARM64 or
              x86_64 <code>.deb</code> to run the native Varve desktop application. This
              is not native ChromeOS Chrome and not an Android app; ChromeOS does not
              expose GPU acceleration to Linux applications.
            </p>
            <p class="route-meta">Best for: testing the native desktop feature set. GUI behavior on a Chromebook is unverified.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="route-caveat">
          There is <strong>no native ChromeOS installer</strong>, and Android, Windows, or
          macOS packages are not substitutes. Do not install an x86_64 package on an ARM
          Chromebook (or vice versa).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Requirements</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Browser route:</strong> a current Chromium-based browser (Chrome or Edge). Chromebooks ship Chrome, so this is always present. Firefox and Safari work with the download save fallback.</li>
          <li><strong>Linux route:</strong> Linux enabled in Settings → About ChromeOS → Developers, an ARM64 or x86_64 Chromebook (Chromebooks are ARM64; Intel/AMD models are x86_64), Debian 12+ inside the container, and roughly 2 GB of free space before installing. The package itself is about 49 MB and installs about 85 MB of files.</li>
          <li><strong>Memory:</strong> 8 GB Chromebooks are the reference target; 4 GB devices are expected to degrade through the adaptive profile rather than fail. Installed RAM is shared with ChromeOS, Android, and the Linux container — it is not what one tab receives.</li>
          <li><strong>No NPU or WebGPU guarantee:</strong> WebGPU is used only after a real adapter and device probe; Canvas2D is the authoritative fallback. The MediaTek NPU is not exposed to browsers or the Linux container and Varve does not use it.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>What is tested, and when</h2>
        <table class="route-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Area</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Status</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Browser: install criteria, offline relaunch, update offer, storage cleanup</td>
              <td>Production <code>/try/</code> artifact, automated browser acceptance, 2026-09-12</td>
              <td>Verified in a desktop browser; ChromeOS device run pending</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Browser: sample document, edit, save, reopen, export (SVG/JPEG/PNG)</td>
              <td>Production artifact, decoded pixel/geometry assertions, 2026-09-12</td>
              <td>Verified in a desktop browser; ChromeOS device run pending</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Responsive layout, touch/pen input, virtual keyboard, back gesture</td>
              <td>13–21 emulated Chromebook viewports and CDP touch/pen events, 2026-09-12</td>
              <td>Regression-tested; hardware behavior unverified</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Linux ARM64 package metadata, checksums, glibc floor, dependency closure</td>
              <td>Published {releaseLabel} artifacts, byte verification and archive resolution, 2026-09-12</td>
              <td>Package verified; Crostini GUI unverified</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Feature comparison</h2>
        <table class="route-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Feature</th>
              <th>Browser tab</th>
              <th>Installed web app</th>
              <th>Linux app</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Core editing (Design, Draw, Photo workspaces, layers, undo)</td>
              <td>Available (bounded demo)</td>
              <td>Same build</td>
              <td>Native application</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Motion, Codegen, Logo, Email workspaces</td>
              <td>Not included</td>
              <td>Not included</td>
              <td>Implemented (device run pending)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Print production (PDF/X, CMYK, ICC)</td>
              <td>Not included</td>
              <td>Not included</td>
              <td>Implemented; CUPS is commonly absent in the container</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>On-device AI models</td>
              <td>Capability-gated, optional; core editing never waits for a download</td>
              <td>Same build</td>
              <td>Implemented; {releaseLabel} AppImage lost its bundled runtime (fix on master), so prefer the .deb</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Files</td>
              <td>Browser storage + File System Access where supported; download fallback</td>
              <td>Same storage as the tab</td>
              <td>Native file dialogs in the Linux container</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Offline</td>
              <td>After one complete online visit</td>
              <td>After one complete online visit</td>
              <td>Local application</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Updates</td>
              <td>New version offered on next visit; reload to apply</td>
              <td>In-app update notice, applies after approval</td>
              <td>Manual: install the newer .deb (no apt repository)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Hardware acceleration</td>
              <td>Canvas2D authoritative; WebGPU only if a device probe succeeds</td>
              <td>Same as the tab</td>
              <td>Not provided by ChromeOS for Linux apps; expect software rendering</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Managed (work or school) Chromebooks</h2>
        <p>
          Administrators control both routes. ChromeOS lets an administrator turn the Linux
          development environment off, and Chrome Enterprise policy can block, allow, or
          force-install web apps. If Linux is missing from Settings, or installation is
          unavailable, the option has been disabled by policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Contact your administrator.</strong> This project does not provide, and
          will not document, a way to bypass managed-device restrictions or ChromeOS
          security controls. The browser route may remain usable even when Linux or app
          installation is blocked; if policy blocks that too, there is no supported route
          on that device.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Set up and first project</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Browser route:</strong> follow the
            <a href={sitePath('/docs/browser-demo')}>Browser Demo &amp; Offline guide</a>.
            It covers installing the web app, offline setup, saving versus recovery,
            storage cleanup, and moving work between browser and desktop.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Linux route:</strong> follow the
            <a href={sitePath('/docs/chromeos-linux')}>ChromeOS Linux guide</a> from
            "Before you start". It includes the architecture check
            (<code>uname -m</code>), checksum verification, install, first launch, and
            uninstall with the data decision.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Either route:</strong> open the sample document, make an edit, save it
            with an explicit destination, close and reopen it, and export once. If any
            step fails, the <a href={sitePath('/support/troubleshooting')}>troubleshooting
            guide</a> covers wrong architecture, missing libraries, blank windows, low
            space, blocked model downloads, and safe diagnostics.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Known limitations, stated plainly</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The browser build is a bounded demo, not the full desktop product.</li>
          <li>No cross-origin isolation is available on the current host, so multi-threaded WASM is unavailable and large documents are slower than on desktop.</li>
          <li>A browser can discard a tab under memory pressure. Recovery copies are a safety net, not a save: keep important work in a real file.</li>
          <li>The Linux route has package-level evidence but no recorded GUI launch on a Chromebook. Rendering, input, fonts, suspend/resume, and printing remain unverified.</li>
          <li>Uninstalling the installed app with "delete data", clearing browsing data, or removing the Linux environment all delete local work. Back up first.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Detailed guides</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/browser-demo')}>Browser Demo &amp; Offline</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/chromeos-linux')}>ChromeOS Linux installation</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/performance')}>Performance on lower-memory devices</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/touch-and-pen')}>Touch, pen, keyboard, and trackpad</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/support/troubleshooting')}>Troubleshooting</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .breadcrumb a,
  .docs-section a,
  .docs-cta a,
  .docs-note a,
  .route-card a {
    color: var(--text-link);
  }

  .docs-page h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
    font-family: var(--font-editorial);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-intro {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .docs-note {
    margin: -1rem 0 3rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .docs-note strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .docs-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section p,
  .docs-section li,
  .docs-section td {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .docs-section p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .docs-section ul,
  .docs-section ol {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .docs-section li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .docs-section strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section code {
    font-size: 0.875em;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-pill);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .route-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .route-card {
    background: var(--surface-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .route-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .route-card p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .route-meta {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .route-badge {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.15em 0.55em;
    margin-left: 0.35rem;
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-accent);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  .route-caveat {
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-surface);
    background: var(--surface-info);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .route-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .route-table th,
  .route-table td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .route-table th {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .route-table td:first-child {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-cta {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 3rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }

  .docs-cta h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-cta p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.9;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .route-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .route-table {
      display: block;
      overflow-x: auto;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }
</style>
